MPN Search Results Cache
Detailed Copilot Implementation Plan
Existing conversation_context.state JSON • No new table/migration • No max-five alternatives cap

1. Objective and non-negotiable decisions
Implement a cache for web-search results used to find alternatives for manufacturer part numbers (MPNs). Reuse the existing conversation_context database row and existing end-of-turn save path. Do not create a new PostgreSQL table, migration, or independent cache persistence mechanism.
Cache entries are scoped to chat_id. On a later turn in the same chat, reuse valid cached entries and search only requested keys that are missing, expired, invalid, or created under an old result version.
Important requirement: there is NO new maximum of five alternatives per MPN. Preserve all alternatives returned by the existing search/ranking pipeline, subject to any limit already imposed by the current provider or product contract. Do not add alternatives[:5] or equivalent truncation.
The cache is an optimization, not the source of truth. Cache parsing or lookup problems must not stop the conversation; fall back to direct search where appropriate.
2. Existing JSON structure and correct placement
The supplied context is an envelope containing top-level turn, state, transcript, and schema_version. Existing conversation fields such as focus, overrides, referents, detections, topic_stack, active_request, pending_clarification, and last_completed_request are nested under state.
{\n  "turn": 2,\n  "state": {\n    "focus": {...},\n    "overrides": [],\n    "referents": {...},\n    "detections": [...],\n    "topic_stack": [...],\n    "active_request": null,\n    "pending_clarification": null,\n    "last_completed_request": null\n  },\n  "transcript": [...],\n  "schema_version": "2.0"\n}

Place webref inside the inner state. If the code variable holds the complete envelope, use context_doc['state']['webref']['alternatives']. If it holds only the inner state, use state['webref']['alternatives']. Copilot must inspect the actual loading code and confirm which shape is passed to each helper before implementing. Do not accidentally add webref at the envelope root.
"state": {\n  "...existing fields...": "...",\n  "webref": {\n    "version": 1,\n    "alternatives": {\n      "LM317T": {\n        "status": "success",\n        "result_version": 1,\n        "fetched_at": "2026-10-14T10:00:00+00:00",\n        "expires_at": "2026-10-15T10:00:00+00:00",\n        "alternatives": [\n          {"mpn": "LM317TG", "manufacturer": "onsemi", "source_url": "https://example.com/part", "confidence": 0.92},\n          {"mpn": "LM317T-TR", "manufacturer": "Example Semi", "source_url": "https://example.com/another-part", "confidence": 0.88}\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}

The example is illustrative. The alternatives array must retain all alternatives returned by the existing pipeline; it is not capped at five. Create webref lazily on the first MPN search so chats that never use this feature remain unchanged.
3. Chat/user isolation
The cache is conversation-scoped, not user-wide. A new chat_id starts with an independent cache even when the same user starts it. A different user or chat must never inherit another chat's entries.
•	Load context using the existing repository/service and chat_id.
•	Verify the authenticated user is authorized to access the chat; apply the existing user_id ownership check.
•	Save to the same context row that was loaded.
•	Confirm the database has a unique constraint/index on chat_id if the code assumes one row per chat.
•	Do not share cache entries across chats as part of this implementation.
4. MPN normalization and cache keys
Use one normalization function in Excel parsing, chat extraction, cache lookup, and cache writes. Normalize before deduplicating.
def normalize_mpn(mpn: str | None) -> str:\n    if not mpn:\n        return ""\n    return "".join(mpn.strip().upper().split())

•	Trim surrounding whitespace, uppercase, and remove whitespace within the key.
•	Preserve hyphens, slashes, dots, and leading zeros; these may distinguish different parts.
•	Reject empty normalized keys.
•	Deduplicate normalized keys for searching, but preserve original input text and Excel row references for output mapping.
•	If manufacturer is necessary to distinguish identical MPN strings, use a composite key such as MANUFACTURER|MPN and normalize both components consistently.
5. Cache entry schema
Use a dictionary keyed by normalized MPN, not a list. Each MPN entry expires and versions independently.
{\n  "status": "success",\n  "result_version": 1,\n  "fetched_at": "ISO-8601 UTC timestamp",\n  "expires_at": "ISO-8601 UTC timestamp",\n  "alternatives": [\n    {\n      "mpn": "candidate MPN",\n      "manufacturer": "manufacturer if available",\n      "source_url": "canonical source URL",\n      "confidence": 0.92\n    }\n  ]\n}

•	Cache only status=success or status=no_alternatives.
•	For success, store all alternatives returned by the current search/ranking pipeline. Do not impose a new per-MPN maximum.
•	For no_alternatives, store an empty alternatives array only when the search completed successfully and genuinely found no alternatives.
•	Never cache timeouts, 429/5xx responses, provider errors, malformed responses, or exceptions as no_alternatives.
•	Store only structured fields needed by the chatbot. Do not store raw HTML, full provider responses, tracking URLs, or verbose exception strings.
6. TTL, versioning, refresh
Start with a configurable 24-hour TTL and RESULT_VERSION=1. Store UTC timestamps in ISO-8601 format and check expiry on every lookup.
•	A valid hit requires a dictionary entry, matching result_version, status success/no_alternatives, a parseable expires_at, and expires_at later than current UTC time.
•	Missing or malformed fields, unknown statuses, malformed timestamps, expired entries, or old result versions count as misses; they must not break the turn.
•	A force-refresh/bypass flag skips cache reuse for requested keys and performs fresh search.
•	If force-refresh fails, preserve the previous entry for future normal reads but report that this turn's refresh failed. Do not claim the old result was freshly verified.
•	Bump RESULT_VERSION when search, ranking, normalization, or cached-result structure changes incompatibly. Do not change conversation schema_version unless existing validation/migration rules require it.
7. Required per-turn flow
1.	Load the conversation context once through the existing code path.
2.	Identify whether the function holds the full context envelope or only inner state, and use the correct webref path.
3.	Extract MPNs from the user's message or uploaded Excel file. Preserve original values and row mappings.
4.	Normalize and deduplicate MPN keys.
5.	Look up only the requested keys in webref.alternatives; do not scan the entire cache to build the LLM prompt.
6.	Classify requested keys as valid hits or misses. Misses include absent, expired, malformed, old-version, and explicitly bypassed entries.
7.	Search only misses, using bounded concurrency and provider-appropriate timeouts/rate limits.
8.	Collect all search results first. Search tasks must return results; they must not mutate shared state or write to the database independently.
9.	Merge cacheable results into the in-memory state once after searches finish. On failed searches, do not overwrite a valid prior entry.
10.	Build a separate turn_results object containing only requested MPNs and their relevant results.
11.	Pass turn_results—not the full webref cache—to the LLM/answer-generation layer.
12.	Use the existing end-of-turn save exactly as the persistence path. Keep existing audit logging behavior.
8. Search concurrency and failure handling
Use a configurable semaphore/concurrency limit and per-search timeout. A starting concurrency of eight is only an example; set it according to the actual search provider limits and existing application patterns.
•	Timeout, rate limiting, HTTP 5xx, invalid provider data, and exceptions are failures—not empty search results.
•	Do not cache failed results as no_alternatives.
•	If an uncached MPN fails, return a failure outcome to the response builder so the user is told that MPN could not be searched.
•	If refresh fails and an old cache entry exists, retain that entry but make the refresh failure visible for the current response.
•	Log safe error categories using existing logging conventions. Do not log credentials, secrets, or large raw provider responses.
•	Ensure async execution and cancellation behavior match the application's existing runtime.
9. State mutation, persistence, concurrency
Single write path
Cache helpers must mutate only the already-loaded in-memory context/state. They must not issue a separate database UPDATE. The existing end-of-turn save persists webref together with all existing state.
Preserve existing state
Do not replace the state object with a cache-only dictionary. Preserve focus, overrides, referents, detections, topic_stack, active_request, pending_clarification, last_completed_request, transcript, turn, and schema_version as managed by existing code. Unknown-key validation must be checked before adding webref.
Concurrent requests
Two simultaneous requests for the same chat can load the same old state and overwrite one another. Inspect existing save logic. If it has no protection, use optimistic concurrency control (compare expected turn/version during save) or an appropriate per-chat lock. On conflict, reload and merge/retry safely or reject a stale save. Never silently overwrite newer state. Confirm the application's turn semantics before relying on turn alone.
10. LLM prompt and token discipline
Stored cache size and prompt size are separate concerns. The cache can contain many MPNs, but the LLM must receive only results for the MPNs requested in the current turn.
requested_keys = normalize_and_deduplicate(extracted_mpns)\nhits, misses = split_hits_misses(inner_state, requested_keys)\nfresh_results = await search_misses(misses, search_fn)\nmerge_results(inner_state, fresh_results)\n\nturn_results = build_turn_results(inner_state, requested_keys, fresh_results)\nanswer = await llm_answer(user_request, mpn_results=turn_results)

Inspect all prompt-building code. If it serializes the full state, explicitly exclude webref.alternatives and pass the selected current-turn results through the intended prompt field. Do not copy the entire cache into transcript or duplicate it in another state field.
11. Cache growth and retention
Remove the proposed max-five alternatives rule entirely. No code path should slice the alternatives array to five. Preserve all alternatives returned by the existing pipeline.
•	Keep entries compact by storing only structured fields used by the chatbot.
•	Prune expired entries when cache updates occur.
•	An overall cap on the number of MPN keys per chat is optional, not required by this plan. Do not add one without approval. If later configured, evict oldest entries by fetched_at and document that this affects reuse, not answer correctness.
•	Measure JSON state size before implementation and after a 100-MPN upload, including tests with many alternatives per MPN.
•	Monitor average/max state size and context load/save latency. Use measured behavior to decide whether further limits are needed.
TTL does not automatically delete a stored entry at expiry. Expired entries become unusable immediately and are physically pruned on a later cache update unless an existing cleanup job is available.
